<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  macOS hardened-runtime entitlements for the signed, notarized build.
  electron-builder passes this file (and, via entitlementsInherit, the same file)
  to `codesign` for every Mach-O in the notarized app.

  This list is deliberately SHORT. Three decisions are load-bearing:

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit — GRANTED. V8 needs a writable-then-executable
    (MAP_JIT) region to JIT JavaScript; under the hardened runtime that requires an
    explicit entitlement. This is the NARROW one.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory — NOT granted, on
    purpose. It is the BROAD sibling of allow-jit and would let any
    unsigned page execute; allow-jit alone is sufficient for modern Electron, so
    granting the broad one only widens the attack surface a notarized app is
    supposed to shrink.

  * App Sandbox (com.apple.security.app-sandbox) — NOT present. The app opens
    user-chosen SQLite files by absolute path anywhere on disk ("Open an
    existing SQLite file… opened in place"); the sandbox's power-scoped file model
    is incompatible with that, and this app ships outside the Mac App Store, so it
    is not required. No sandbox means no need for user-selected-file entitlements
    either — the app already has ordinary file access.

  * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — GRANTED, and it is the
    widest thing in this file. The app opens a project folder and serves it, and
    a project's own packages bring libraries that are signed ad hoc or by someone
    else: SQLite's, argon2's, Tailwind's and lightningcss's native parts. Under
    the hardened runtime every one of them is refused without this key (seen on
    the notarized build: no Tailwind, no database driver from a project's
    node_modules). The app's OWN native addons do not need it: they are
    co-signed with the app's Developer ID and pass strict validation.

    What it costs, said plainly: this process will load a library nobody at
    Adminium signed. What stands in front of that is not a signature but a
    question: a folder's code and libraries load only after the person said they
    trust that folder. And because the app's program also runs as Node when
    asked to, any other program this person runs can start it that way; the key
    store therefore protects a key at rest, not from the person's own programs.

    One file serves the app and every helper (entitlementsInherit), so the
    renderers carry the key too. They load no project library; a second, narrower
    file for them is possible and is not done here.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
